A||||12||deceler8||Lower dash corner repairs||||11||
Z||000000||deceler8||09-28-1999||12:20 PM||||My '86 2m4 has the grey interior and the lower parts of the dash near the doors (by vents) have tears in the vinyl and some of the material has been rubbed down to the foam. Is this repairable, and how ?<P>Has anyone tackled this and if so, any advice? Rest of the dash is in good shape.<P>Thanks again for the advice.||||reg||11
Z||000001||lowCG||09-28-1999||01:27 PM||||Well,I haven't done it yet,but I plan on trying some urethane caulk,using tape to hold it until dry,then painted with some matching Mar-Hyde(vinyl paint),not sure about adhesion of the paint to this,need to try it first.<BR>Bondo might work too,then painted.||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||09-28-1999||09:53 PM||||I have a hunk missing out of one my Batmobile's lower dash corner too. My 85 GT has cracks in both of those corners. It seems like most the salvage yard Fieros are damaged there too. Those corners must really catch hell. Keep us posted on your progress, if your repair works reasonably well, I'm going to try it too. I wish somebody made good reproduction dash parts, for a cheap price.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Cliff Pennock||09-29-1999||03:01 AM||||I had tried all kinds of materials to repair it, and found only one that will do the job. I'm not sure what it's called but it's a polyester paste that, when mixed with a hardener, becomes solid. I've got this stuff at the office so I'll check what it's called when I get there. You can also get this stuff with fiberglass in it, which I suspect is even better.||||reg||1
Z||000004||deceler8||09-29-1999||09:06 AM||||A while back, I inherited a vinyl repair kit with all kinds of colors that come in a paste that can be mixed to approximate nearly any color. It also comes with textured paper to match the texture of the vinyl and hardens when heat is applied. I'm gonna give this a shot and see how it works on an inconspicous area and I'll report the results.<P>Just wondered what others had done and whether there would be an easy fix. Easy as in not having to remove the dash.||||reg||10
Z||000005||BlackGT||09-29-1999||09:44 AM||||I really would like to know what that stuff is called since I too have the same issue,<BR>Keep us posted!||||reg||6
Z||000006||Cliff Pennock||09-29-1999||12:12 PM||||Sorry guys, y'all have to wait until tomorrow. I'm home now and forgot to check what this stuff is called...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><P>BTW, I fixed that corner about two years ago, and it still holds.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Carrolles||09-29-1999||12:22 PM||||Hi,<P>Some time back (maybe 2 years ago) I remember seeing an ad for replacement pieces for repairing this type of damage.  I'll hunt around and see if I can find the link.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Clint||09-29-1999||05:16 PM||||Didn't Ed Parks have a fix that he was selling? aka Fiero Factory<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||lowCG||09-30-1999||12:41 AM||||A product called Duraglass,which seems to be more like fiberglass resin with some very short glass fibers,yet spreads like a paste,and is very strong in thin applications,would be better than Bondo,I would think,after hearing what Cliff descrbed using on his car.<BR>Would need to be put on carefully,or cut before it sets up all the way though.||||reg||12
Z||000010||william_thorniii||02-14-2000||07:19 PM||||Rodney Dickman is going to be working on finishing off a solution to this problem.  Ed parks began working on a solution, but never got it to market.  Rodney has taken over and is trying to have a finished product by the end of summer.  This product will be made of vinyl and colored to match the misc. gray's and browns.<P>Otherwise, I have heard of a paper mache technique.  This method (I have not tried) uses tissue and fiberglass hardener to replace and shape the material there.  It sounds difficult to me, but again I have never tried it.  This method was told to the fiero-list by Eric Schneck (I think)... so any of you listers that want more information contact him.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||10
Z||000011||RZURCTN||02-15-2000||09:29 AM||||FieroDave did a repair to one of his Fiero's.  It involved a putty that comes in a roll.  You cut off a piece, knead it and apply it.  It hardens and Whalla!  I have a similar product here at work called "PIG" repair putty.  It's an epoxy stick made by New Pig Corporation.  You can call 1-800-HOT-HOGS (468-4647) for prices, ect.  Or check out your local hardware store for a similar product.  I haven't gotten around to doing mine yet, but you could talk to FieroDave about his results.  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||fierodave||02-16-2000||04:12 AM||||       DASH CORNER REPAIRS<BR>Yeppers no expensive repairs for me!<P>One of the putty sticks is about $4.99  <BR>it will do three dash corners(DEPENDS ON DAMAGE SIZE)<P>If you look real close at the INDY resto page on my site you will see the drivers corner WAS GONE! not anymore!  <BR>First you take a razor blade and remove all the broken vinyl<BR>(remove vent too)  I cut it down to the dash under the foam.  <BR>Make straight line cuts,  then remove the foam from behind the cuts at least 1/4 inch in, <P>then I take 36 grit and rough up the vinyl around the removed section 1/4" at least! <BR>better to go 1/2 inch around the affected area!<P>OK now  eyeball enough putty to fill the 1/4" areas that the foam is gone in, then apply it, pushing it in, and smooth it with your finger.<P>wait 15 minutes then rough what you can get at with the 36 again!<P>then eyeball another piece and layer it from one end of the area gone to the other, keep in mind the countour<BR>of the dash corner.<P>let sit 15 minutes(longer will make it harder to sand off)  then apply the final coat of putty  make it higher than the finished corner will be(higher meaning leave some extra all around the area)  then 15 minutes <BR>36 it until you have the correct countour you like!  Now  go over lightly with 80 grit......then 220 grit,  now you don't want it to smooth or it will not match the dash grain,  here is a little trick I use at this stage.  <BR>I take a small awl and scribe in some dash texture, random little scratches that look like the dash grain, but!!!  make it bolder than the dash because you will go over it again with 220 grit to take out the deep ones,  remember also in the first stages of 36 to feather in the hardend putty to the dash vinyl, with practice you can do a corner in an hour or so,  my best time was 20 minutes because I can do one coaters now!  no layers lol <BR>but if it is your first time do it as described to give yourself a chance to see how fast the stuff works.<BR>If you want to call me I would be happy to go over it on the phone with you!  <P>The reason I do it like this is the local dash guy gets $60.00 per side and that is way too much,  and if your asking ~~~~~  hey!!!!!!!  the putty is hard  if you hit it with your knee won't it  crack it? again~~~~~~   Yes!!  but if you crack this stuff the original dash would have been  too!!!!  <P>I do have a two year old repair still going strong. <P>I will look up the name of the stuff tomorrow at the shop and post it.  <P>I keep it around at all costs   <BR>I have done 20 plus corners with it and no one knew the diff(unless I pointed it out)  <P>But that depends on your painting skills with a brush and eye for matching the colors!  <P>I use spray can paint  mix a few colors together then apply color, and dab it with my finger until dull like the dash  :~)  Phone # is in the profile section. email first to see if I am around OK.<BR>sorry for the novel!! LOL<BR>FD<P>OK  it is called "WELD STIK"<BR> By LOCTITE<BR>FD<p>[This message has been edited by fierodave (edited 02-17-2000).]||||reg||10
